{"task_id": "justlogic_1552", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B", "error_type": "incorrect_reasoning", "k": 1, "length_level": "short", "seed": 42, "variant": "recovery", "extracted_answer": "Uncertain", "gold_answer": "True", "correct": false, "verifier_score": 0, "judge_output": "Incorrect", "judge_raw_path": "data/judge_raw/dialogue/justlogic_1552.jsonl", "det_correct": false, "judge_fallback": false, "tokens_generated": 1010, "finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 573, "raw_output": "Okay, so I'm trying to figure out whether the statement \"The claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false\" is True, False, or Uncertain based on the given premises. Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's look at the premises one by one:\n\n1. **Premise 1:** \"Either if venous drainage follows the main arterial supply, then beavers live for years or plants need food.\" This seems to be a conditional statement where if venous drainage follows the main arterial supply, then one of two things happens: either beavers live for years or plants need food. So, if venous drainage follows the main arterial supply, at least one of those two outcomes is true.\n\n2. **Premise 2:** \"One may reasonably assume that if if venous drainage follows the main arterial supply, then beavers live for years, then the claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false.\" This is a bit complex. It says that assuming the first part (venous drainage following main arterial supply leading to beavers living for years), we can conclude that the claim about plants with underground rhizomes making a dayflower a herb is false.\n\n3. **Premise 3:** \"There is good reason to believe that either earthworms have no lungs, and absorb oxygen directly through their skin or bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside.\" This presents two possibilities: either earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen through their skin, or bombs are more likely planted outside than inside. It doesn't specify which one is true, just that one of them is.\n\n4. **Premise 4:** \"If earthworms have no lungs, and absorb oxygen directly through their skin, then if plants need food, then the claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false.\" This links the first alternative in Premise 3 (earthworms' breathing) to the same claim about dayflowers, but only if plants need food.\n\n5. **Premise 5:** \"If it should be the case that bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside, it follows that if plants need food, then the claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false.\" This links the second alternative in Premise 3 (bomb placement) to the same claim about dayflowers, again conditional on plants needing food.\n\nNow, the statement we need to evaluate is: \"The claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false.\"\n\nSo, to determine if this claim is false, we need to see if the premises lead us to conclude that the implication \"if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb\" is false.\n\nLet's see how the premises connect to this.\n\nFrom Premise 2, if we assume that venous drainage follows the main arterial supply leading to beavers living for years, then the dayflower claim is false. But Premise 1 says that if venous drainage follows the main arterial supply, then either beavers live for years or plants need food. So, if we have venous drainage following the main arterial supply, one of those two things is true. If beavers live for years is true, then from Premise 2, the dayflower claim is false. If plants need food is true, then from Premise 4 or 5, depending on which alternative in Premise 3 is true, the dayflower claim is also false.\n\nBut Premise 3 says that either earthworms have no lungs or bombs are more likely planted outside. It doesn't tell us which one is true, but both paths lead to the dayflower claim being false if plants need food.\n\nHowever, none of the premises directly tell us whether plants actually need food or not. So, even though if plants need food, the dayflower claim is false, we don't know if plants need food in the first place. Therefore, we can't definitively conclude that the dayflower claim is false because it depends on an unknown condition.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the premises set up a chain of reasoning where the dayflower claim being false depends on plants needing food, but since we don't know if plants need food, we can't determine the truth of the dayflower claim.\n</think>\n\nThe claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is uncertain based on the given premises. This is because the premises establish a conditional relationship where the claim's falsity depends on plants needing food, but there is no direct assertion that plants indeed need food. Therefore, the truth value of the dayflower claim remains undetermined.\n\nFinal answer: Uncertain", "insertion_step": 3, "domain": "logic", "eval_type": "truth_value", "dialogue_id": "20423687-fc5d-45f1-9c4d-dcea4487ff6e", "n_rounds": 1, "condition": "adversarial", "turn2_source": "baseline_prefix", "dialogue_mode": "adaptive_alternating", "recovery_terminated_by": "natural"}
{"task_id": "justlogic_1552", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B", "error_type": "incorrect_reasoning", "k": 1, "length_level": "long", "seed": 42, "variant": "stop", "extracted_answer": "Uncertain", "gold_answer": "True", "correct": false, "verifier_score": 0, "judge_output": "Let me trace through the logic carefully.\n\nLet me define:\n- A: venous drainage follows the main arterial supply\n- B: beavers live for years\n- C: plants need food\n- D: if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb (i.e., D = \"plants possess underground rhizomes → dayflower is a herb\")\n- E: earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen directly through their skin\n- F: bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside\n\nPremises:\n1. (A→B) ∨ C\n2. (A→B) → ¬D\n3. E ∨ F\n4. E → (C → ¬D)\n5. F → (C → ¬D)\n\nFrom premise 3: E ∨ F\nFrom", "judge_raw_path": "data/judge_raw/dialogue/justlogic_1552.jsonl", "det_correct": false, "judge_fallback": true, "tokens_generated": 3, "finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 822, "raw_output": "\n\nFinal answer:  Uncertain", "insertion_step": 3, "domain": "logic", "eval_type": "truth_value", "dialogue_id": "7ad75931-6259-4eba-b6c1-4fb375c7b6f2", "n_rounds": 1, "condition": "adversarial", "turn2_source": "baseline_prefix", "dialogue_mode": "adaptive_alternating"}
{"task_id": "justlogic_1552", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B", "error_type": "incorrect_reasoning", "k": 1, "length_level": "long", "seed": 42, "variant": "recovery", "extracted_answer": "Uncertain", "gold_answer": "True", "correct": false, "verifier_score": 0, "judge_output": "Incorrect", "judge_raw_path": "data/judge_raw/dialogue/justlogic_1552.jsonl", "det_correct": false, "judge_fallback": false, "tokens_generated": 1039, "finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 820, "raw_output": "Okay, so I'm trying to figure out whether the statement \"The claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false\" is True, False, or Uncertain based on the given premises. Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's list out the premises to keep things clear:\n\n1. If venous drainage follows the main arterial supply, then either beavers live for years or plants need food.\n2. If the first premise's condition is true (i.e., if venous drainage follows the main arterial supply), then the claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false.\n3. Either earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen directly through their skin, or bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside.\n4. If earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen directly through their skin, then if plants need food, the claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false.\n5. If bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside, then if plants need food, the claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false.\n\nSo, the statement we need to evaluate is about the truth value of the claim regarding plants with underground rhizomes and dayflowers being herbs. Let's denote this claim as C: If plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb.\n\nFrom the premises, it seems that the falsity of C is tied to whether \"plants need food\" is true, and that depends on other conditions. Let's map out the dependencies:\n\n- Premise 1: Venous drainage following the main arterial supply → (Beavers live for years ∨ Plants need food)\n- Premise 2: Venous drainage following the main arterial supply → ¬C\n- Premise 3: Earthworms have no lungs ∨ Bombs are more likely outside\n- Premise 4: Earthworms have no lungs → (Plants need food → ¬C)\n- Premise 5: Bombs are more likely outside → (Plants need food → ¬C)\n\nSo, the key here is whether \"plants need food\" is true because both Premise 4 and 5 have \"Plants need food\" as a condition to conclude ¬C.\n\nBut Premise 1 tells us that if venous drainage follows the main arterial supply, then either beavers live for years or plants need food. However, Premise 1 doesn't tell us whether venous drainage actually follows the main arterial supply. It's a conditional statement, not an assertion of the antecedent. So, we don't know if \"venous drainage follows the main arterial supply\" is true or false.\n\nIf we assume that venous drainage does follow the main arterial supply, then Premise 1 would imply that either beavers live for years or plants need food. But since we don't know if the antecedent is true, we can't definitively say that either beavers live for years or plants need food.\n\nMoreover, Premise 3 is a disjunction: either earthworms have no lungs or bombs are more likely outside. But we don't have any information to determine which one is true. It could be that earthworms have no lungs, or it could be that bombs are more likely outside, or even both. Without knowing which one is the case, we can't proceed further.\n\nIf we could determine that \"earthworms have no lungs\" is true, then Premise 4 would come into play, linking \"plants need food\" to ¬C. Similarly, if \"bombs are more likely outside\" is true, Premise 5 would link \"plants need food\" to ¬C. But since we don't know which disjunct is true, we can't activate either Premise 4 or 5.\n\nAdditionally, even if we could establish \"plants need food,\" we would still need to know if \"venous drainage follows the main arterial supply\" to use Premise 2, which directly links to ¬C. But again, we don't have information about the antecedent in Premise 1.\n\nSo, all paths to determining ¬C rely on conditions that aren't confirmed by the premises. We don't know if venous drainage follows the main arterial supply, we don't know if earthworms have no lungs or if bombs are more likely outside, and consequently, we can't establish whether \"plants need food\" is true. Without that, we can't conclude anything about the truth value of C.\n\nTherefore, based solely on the premises provided, it's impossible to determine whether the statement \"The claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false\" is True, False, or Uncertain. All the necessary conditions to confirm or deny the statement remain unverified.\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Uncertain", "insertion_step": 3, "domain": "logic", "eval_type": "truth_value", "dialogue_id": "7ad75931-6259-4eba-b6c1-4fb375c7b6f2", "n_rounds": 1, "condition": "adversarial", "turn2_source": "baseline_prefix", "dialogue_mode": "adaptive_alternating", "recovery_terminated_by": "natural"}
{"task_id": "justlogic_1552", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B", "error_type": "incorrect_reasoning", "k": 1, "length_level": "short", "seed": 43, "variant": "stop", "extracted_answer": "Uncertain", "gold_answer": "True", "correct": false, "verifier_score": 0, "judge_output": "Let me trace through the logic carefully.\n\nLet me define:\n- A: venous drainage follows the main arterial supply\n- B: beavers live for years\n- C: plants need food\n- D: earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen directly through their skin\n- E: bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside\n- F: if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb (so ¬F means this is false)\n\nPremises:\n1. (A→B) ∨ C\n2. (A→B) → ¬F\n3. D ∨ E\n4. D → (C → ¬F)\n5. E → (C → ¬F)\n\nFrom premise 3: D ∨ E\nFrom premises 4 and 5: In either case", "judge_raw_path": "data/judge_raw/dialogue/justlogic_1552.jsonl", "det_correct": false, "judge_fallback": true, "tokens_generated": 3, "finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 575, "raw_output": "\n\nFinal answer:  Uncertain", "insertion_step": 3, "domain": "logic", "eval_type": "truth_value", "dialogue_id": "03e49ec0-a12b-4f8e-a124-97b68f772ed6", "n_rounds": 1, "condition": "adversarial", "turn2_source": "baseline_prefix", "dialogue_mode": "adaptive_alternating"}
{"task_id": "justlogic_1552", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B", "error_type": "incorrect_reasoning", "k": 1, "length_level": "short", "seed": 43, "variant": "recovery", "extracted_answer": "Uncertain", "gold_answer": "True", "correct": false, "verifier_score": 0, "judge_output": "Incorrect", "judge_raw_path": "data/judge_raw/dialogue/justlogic_1552.jsonl", "det_correct": false, "judge_fallback": false, "tokens_generated": 910, "finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 573, "raw_output": "Alright, so I'm trying to figure out whether the statement \"The claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false\" is True, False, or Uncertain based on the given premises. Okay, let's take this step by step.\n\nFirst, I need to understand the premises clearly. There are four premises provided:\n\n1. If venous drainage follows the main arterial supply, then beavers live for years or plants need food.\n2. If the first premise's condition is true, then the claim about dayflowers is false.\n3. Either earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen directly through their skin, or bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside.\n4. If earthworms have no lungs, and absorb oxygen directly through their skin, then if plants need food, the dayflower claim is false.\n5. If bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside, then if plants need food, the dayflower claim is false.\n\nOkay, so the first thing I notice is that premise 3 is a disjunction—it presents two possibilities: either earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen directly, or bombs are more likely to be planted outside. But it doesn't specify which one is actually true. Both options could lead to the same conclusion about the dayflower claim being false if plants need food.\n\nNow, looking at premises 4 and 5, both of them have a conditional structure. If either of the two options in premise 3 is true, and if plants need food, then the dayflower claim is false. So, it seems like the truth of the dayflower claim depends on two things: one of the options in premise 3 being true and plants needing food.\n\nBut wait, premise 1 also ties into this. It says that if venous drainage follows the main arterial supply, then either beavers live for years or plants need food. So, if we accept that venous drainage follows the main arterial supply, then we have two possibilities: beavers live for years or plants need food. If plants need food, that could lead to the dayflower claim being false based on premises 4 and 5.\n\nHowever, there's an issue here. Premise 1 only tells us that if venous drainage follows the main arterial supply, then either beavers live for years or plants need food. It doesn't confirm whether venous drainage does follow the main arterial supply. So, we don't know if this condition is even met. If venous drainage doesn't follow the main arterial supply, then premise 1 doesn't tell us anything about beavers or plants needing food.\n\nSimilarly, premise 3 doesn't tell us which of the two options is true. We don't know if earthworms have no lungs or if bombs are more likely to be planted outside. Both scenarios could potentially lead to the dayflower claim being false if plants need food, but without knowing which one is true, it's hard to make a definite conclusion.\n\nAnother thing to consider is whether plants actually need food. None of the premises directly state that plants need food as a fact. Premise 1 only says that if venous drainage follows the main arterial supply, then either beavers live for years or plants need food. So, even if we accept that condition, we still don't know if plants need food or not—it could be that beavers live for years instead.\n\nWithout knowing whether plants need food, we can't definitively say whether the dayflower claim is false. The chain of reasoning relies on plants needing food to conclude that the dayflower claim is false, but since we don't have confirmation that plants need food, the conclusion remains uncertain.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the premises set up a series of conditional statements, but they don't provide enough information to confirm all the necessary conditions to definitively determine the truth of the dayflower claim. There are too many unknowns and dependencies on unconfirmed facts, which means we can't be certain whether the claim is false or not based solely on the given premises.\n</think>\n\nBased on the analysis, the statement \"The claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false\" is **Uncertain**. The premises establish a series of conditional relationships but lack sufficient information to confirm all necessary conditions, leaving the truth of the claim undetermined.\n\nFinal answer: Uncertain", "insertion_step": 3, "domain": "logic", "eval_type": "truth_value", "dialogue_id": "03e49ec0-a12b-4f8e-a124-97b68f772ed6", "n_rounds": 1, "condition": "adversarial", "turn2_source": "baseline_prefix", "dialogue_mode": "adaptive_alternating", "recovery_terminated_by": "natural"}
{"task_id": "justlogic_1552", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B", "error_type": "incorrect_reasoning", "k": 1, "length_level": "long", "seed": 43, "variant": "stop", "extracted_answer": "Uncertain", "gold_answer": "True", "correct": false, "verifier_score": 0, "judge_output": "Let me trace through the logic carefully.\n\nLet me define:\n- A: venous drainage follows the main arterial supply\n- B: beavers live for years\n- C: plants need food\n- D: earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen directly through their skin\n- E: bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside\n- F: if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb (so ¬F means this is false)\n\nPremises:\n1. (A→B) ∨ C\n2. (A→B) → ¬F\n3. D ∨ E\n4. D → (C → ¬F)\n5. E → (C → ¬F)\n\nFrom premise 3: D ∨ E\nFrom premises 4 and 5: In either case", "judge_raw_path": "data/judge_raw/dialogue/justlogic_1552.jsonl", "det_correct": false, "judge_fallback": true, "tokens_generated": 3, "finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 822, "raw_output": "\n\nFinal answer:  Uncertain", "insertion_step": 3, "domain": "logic", "eval_type": "truth_value", "dialogue_id": "a17fb16e-c617-448e-83c0-fe1d9c866193", "n_rounds": 1, "condition": "adversarial", "turn2_source": "baseline_prefix", "dialogue_mode": "adaptive_alternating"}
{"task_id": "justlogic_1552", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B", "error_type": "incorrect_reasoning", "k": 1, "length_level": "long", "seed": 43, "variant": "recovery", "extracted_answer": "Uncertain", "gold_answer": "True", "correct": false, "verifier_score": 0, "judge_output": "Incorrect", "judge_raw_path": "data/judge_raw/dialogue/justlogic_1552.jsonl", "det_correct": false, "judge_fallback": false, "tokens_generated": 727, "finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 820, "raw_output": "Alright, so I'm trying to figure out whether the claim that \"if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb\" is false based on the premises provided. Okay, let's take this step by step.\n\nFirst, I need to understand the premises one by one and see how they connect. The first premise says that if venous drainage follows the main arterial supply, then either beavers live for years or plants need food. So, this is a conditional statement: if A, then B or C. But I don't know if A is true or not. It just tells me that if A happens, then one of B or C happens.\n\nMoving on to the second premise, it says that if the first premise's condition is true (meaning if A is true), then the claim that \"if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb\" is false. So, this is another conditional: if A, then not D, where D is the dayflower claim.\n\nThe third premise presents an either/or situation: either earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen directly through their skin, or bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside. This is a disjunction: either E or F.\n\nPremise four says that if earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen directly through their skin (E), then if plants need food (C), the dayflower claim (D) is false. So, if E is true, then C leads to not D.\n\nPremise five states that if bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside than inside (F), then if plants need food (C), the dayflower claim (D) is false. So, if F is true, then C also leads to not D.\n\nNow, I need to see if I can establish that the dayflower claim is false. Let's see:\n\n1. From premise one, if A is true, then either B or C is true. But I don't know if A is true.\n\n2. If A is true, then from premise two, not D is true.\n\n3. Additionally, if E is true, then from premise four, if C is true, not D is true.\n\n4. If F is true, then from premise five, if C is true, not D is true.\n\nBut I don't know whether E or F is true. The third premise only tells me that either E or F is true, but not which one. So, I can't be sure whether E or F is the case.\n\nFurthermore, I don't know if A is true. Premise one is a conditional; it doesn't assert that A is actually happening. So, I can't assume that B or C is true unless I know A is true.\n\nIf I can't establish whether A is true, then I can't confirm whether B or C is true. And if I can't confirm whether C is true, then even if E or F were true, I still wouldn't know if C is true to trigger the consequence in premises four and five.\n\nSince I don't know if A is true, and I don't know whether E or F is true, I can't definitively say whether the dayflower claim is false. All the pathways to establishing not D depend on uncertain premises.\n\nTherefore, based solely on the given premises, it's impossible to determine with certainty whether the dayflower claim is false. There's not enough information to confirm or deny it definitively.\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Uncertain", "insertion_step": 3, "domain": "logic", "eval_type": "truth_value", "dialogue_id": "a17fb16e-c617-448e-83c0-fe1d9c866193", "n_rounds": 1, "condition": "adversarial", "turn2_source": "baseline_prefix", "dialogue_mode": "adaptive_alternating", "recovery_terminated_by": "natural"}
