{"task_id": "justlogic_1552", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B", "error_type": "incorrect_reasoning", "k": 1, "length_level": "short", "seed": 46, "variant": "recovery", "extracted_answer": "Uncertain", "gold_answer": "True", "correct": false, "verifier_score": 0, "judge_output": "Incorrect", "judge_raw_path": "data/judge_raw/dialogue/justlogic_1552.jsonl", "det_correct": false, "judge_fallback": false, "tokens_generated": 1006, "finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 573, "raw_output": "Okay, so I'm trying to figure out whether the statement \"The claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false\" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given premises. Let me go through this step by step.\n\nFirst, let's list out the premises again to make sure I understand them correctly:\n\n1. If venous drainage follows the main arterial supply, then either beavers live for years or plants need food.\n2. If the first premise's condition is true, then the claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false.\n3. Either earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen directly through their skin, or bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside.\n4. If earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen directly through their skin, then if plants need food, then the claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false.\n5. If bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside, it follows that if plants need food, then the claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false.\n\nNow, I need to determine if the statement \"The claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false\" is true, false, or uncertain based on these premises.\n\nLet me try to break down the logical structure here. It seems like a series of conditional statements and disjunctions. Maybe I can represent them symbolically to make it clearer.\n\nLet me assign some variables:\n\n- Let V represent \"venous drainage follows the main arterial supply.\"\n- Let B represent \"beavers live for years.\"\n- Let P represent \"plants need food.\"\n- Let E represent \"earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen directly through their skin.\"\n- Let Bo represent \"bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside.\"\n- Let D represent \"if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb.\"\n\nNow, translating the premises into logical expressions:\n\n1. V → (B ∨ P)\n2. V → ¬D\n3. E ∨ Bo\n4. E → (P → ¬D)\n5. Bo → (P → ¬D)\n\nAnd the statement we need to evaluate is ¬D.\n\nOkay, so from premise 1: If V is true, then either B or P is true. From premise 2: If V is true, then ¬D is true. Premise 3: Either E or Bo is true. Premises 4 and 5 both say that if E or Bo is true, respectively, then if P is true, ¬D is true.\n\nSo, if we can establish that P is true, then regardless of whether E or Bo is true, ¬D would follow. But do we have any information that confirms P is true?\n\nLooking at the premises, I don't see any that directly assert P. Premise 1 says that if V is true, then either B or P is true, but it doesn't tell us whether V is true or not. Similarly, premises 4 and 5 only tell us that if E or Bo is true, and if P is true, then ¬D is true, but they don't confirm whether P is true.\n\nSo, unless we can confirm P is true, we can't definitively conclude ¬D. Since P's truth isn't established by any of the premises, the statement ¬D remains uncertain.\n\nWait, but premise 2 says that if V is true, then ¬D is true. So if V is true, we can directly conclude ¬D. But do we know if V is true? The premises don't tell us that. They just give us conditional statements.\n\nSimilarly, premise 3 gives us that either E or Bo is true, but it doesn't specify which one. So, even if E or Bo is true, without knowing P's truth, we can't determine ¬D.\n\nTherefore, since the premises don't provide enough information to confirm P or V, the truth of ¬D can't be determined definitively. It might be true under certain conditions, but we don't have enough information to confirm it universally.\n\nSo, I think the statement is uncertain based on the given premises.\n</think>\n\nBased on the analysis of the premises, the statement \"The claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false\" cannot be definitively determined as true or false. The premises establish conditional relationships and disjunctions but do not provide sufficient information to confirm the necessary antecedents (such as whether plants need food or if venous drainage follows the main arterial supply). Therefore, the truth value of the statement remains uncertain.\n\nFinal answer: Uncertain", "insertion_step": 3, "domain": "logic", "eval_type": "truth_value", "dialogue_id": "a0d37089-4779-4b18-a25b-2d16c234ac58", "n_rounds": 1, "condition": "adversarial", "turn2_source": "baseline_prefix", "dialogue_mode": "adaptive_alternating", "recovery_terminated_by": "natural"}
{"task_id": "justlogic_1552", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B", "error_type": "incorrect_reasoning", "k": 1, "length_level": "long", "seed": 46, "variant": "stop", "extracted_answer": "Uncertain", "gold_answer": "True", "correct": false, "verifier_score": 0, "judge_output": "Let me trace through the logic carefully.\n\nLet me define:\n- A: venous drainage follows the main arterial supply\n- B: beavers live for years\n- C: plants need food\n- D: if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb (i.e., D = \"plants possess underground rhizomes → dayflower is a herb\")\n- E: earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen directly through their skin\n- F: bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside\n\nPremises:\n1. (A→B) ∨ C\n2. (A→B) → ¬D\n3. E ∨ F\n4. E → (C → ¬D)\n5. F → (C → ¬D)\n\nFrom premise 3: E ∨ F\nFrom", "judge_raw_path": "data/judge_raw/dialogue/justlogic_1552.jsonl", "det_correct": false, "judge_fallback": true, "tokens_generated": 3, "finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 822, "raw_output": "\n\nFinal answer:  Uncertain", "insertion_step": 3, "domain": "logic", "eval_type": "truth_value", "dialogue_id": "333f2775-c364-4082-ba32-90a0308aa048", "n_rounds": 1, "condition": "adversarial", "turn2_source": "baseline_prefix", "dialogue_mode": "adaptive_alternating"}
{"task_id": "justlogic_1552", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B", "error_type": "incorrect_reasoning", "k": 1, "length_level": "long", "seed": 46, "variant": "recovery", "extracted_answer": "Uncertain", "gold_answer": "True", "correct": false, "verifier_score": 0, "judge_output": "Incorrect", "judge_raw_path": "data/judge_raw/dialogue/justlogic_1552.jsonl", "det_correct": false, "judge_fallback": false, "tokens_generated": 712, "finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 820, "raw_output": "Okay, so I'm trying to figure out whether the statement \"The claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false\" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given premises. Let me go through this step by step.\n\nFirst, let's list out the premises again to make sure I understand them correctly:\n\n1. If venous drainage follows the main arterial supply, then either beavers live for years or plants need food.\n2. If the first premise's condition is true, then the claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false.\n3. Either earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen directly through their skin, or bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside.\n4. If earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen directly through their skin, then if plants need food, then the claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false.\n5. If bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside, then if plants need food, then the claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false.\n\nOkay, so the statement we need to evaluate is whether the claim \"if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb\" is false. Let's call this claim C for simplicity.\n\nNow, looking at premise 2, it says that if the first premise's condition is true, then claim C is false. The first premise's condition is \"venous drainage follows the main arterial supply.\" So, if that's true, then C is false. But we don't know if venous drainage actually follows the main arterial supply. It's just a conditional statement.\n\nPremise 3 is a disjunction: either earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen through their skin, or bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside. Let's call these two options A and B respectively.\n\nPremise 4 says if A is true, then if plants need food, then C is false. Premise 5 says if B is true, then if plants need food, then C is false.\n\nSo, both A and B, if true, lead to C being false provided that plants need food.\n\nBut wait, do we know if plants need food? Let's see. The first premise says if venous drainage follows the main arterial supply, then either beavers live for years or plants need food. So, if venous drainage follows, then either beavers live for years or plants need food. But we don't know if venous drainage actually follows, so we don't know if either of those outcomes are true.\n\nMoreover, even if we could establish that plants need food, we would still need to know whether A or B is true to trigger the consequence that C is false. But premise 3 only tells us that either A or B is true, not which one. So we don't know which path to take.\n\nTherefore, since we don't know if venous drainage follows, we can't establish that plants need food. And even if we could, we still don't know whether A or B is true, so we can't definitively say that C is false.\n\nTherefore, the statement that claim C is false cannot be determined from the premises alone. It's uncertain.\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Uncertain", "insertion_step": 3, "domain": "logic", "eval_type": "truth_value", "dialogue_id": "333f2775-c364-4082-ba32-90a0308aa048", "n_rounds": 1, "condition": "adversarial", "turn2_source": "baseline_prefix", "dialogue_mode": "adaptive_alternating", "recovery_terminated_by": "natural"}
{"task_id": "justlogic_1552", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B", "error_type": "paraphrased_shift", "k": 1, "length_level": "short", "seed": 42, "variant": "stop", "extracted_answer": "True", "gold_answer": "True", "correct": true, "verifier_score": 1, "judge_output": "Correct", "judge_raw_path": "data/judge_raw/dialogue/justlogic_1552.jsonl", "det_correct": true, "judge_fallback": false, "tokens_generated": 2, "finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 615, "raw_output": "\n\nFinal answer:  True", "insertion_step": 3, "domain": "logic", "eval_type": "truth_value", "dialogue_id": "122ef7b9-e56d-4367-ba9e-3841fc87bd15", "n_rounds": 1, "condition": "adversarial", "turn2_source": "baseline_prefix", "dialogue_mode": "adaptive_alternating"}
{"task_id": "justlogic_1552", "model": "deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B", "error_type": "paraphrased_shift", "k": 1, "length_level": "short", "seed": 42, "variant": "recovery", "extracted_answer": "Uncertain", "gold_answer": "True", "correct": false, "verifier_score": 0, "judge_output": "Incorrect", "judge_raw_path": "data/judge_raw/dialogue/justlogic_1552.jsonl", "det_correct": false, "judge_fallback": false, "tokens_generated": 799, "finish_reason": "stop", "prompt_tokens": 613, "raw_output": "Okay, so I'm trying to figure out whether the statement \"The claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false\" is True, False, or Uncertain based on the given premises. Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let's list out the premises to keep things clear:\n\n1. If venous drainage follows the main arterial supply, then either beavers live for years or plants need food.\n2. If the first premise is true, then the claim that if plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb is false.\n3. Either earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen directly through their skin, or bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside.\n4. If earthworms have no lungs and absorb oxygen directly through their skin, then if plants need food, the dayflower claim is false.\n5. If bombs are three times more likely to be planted outside buildings than inside, then if plants need food, the dayflower claim is false.\n\nAlright, so the goal is to determine whether the dayflower claim is false. Let's define the dayflower claim as D: If plants possess underground rhizomes, then a dayflower is a herb.\n\nFrom premise 2, if the first premise (P1) is true, then D is false. So, if P1 is true, then ¬D.\n\nP1 says: If venous drainage follows the main arterial supply (let's call this A), then either beavers live for years (B) or plants need food (F). So, P1 is A → (B ∨ F).\n\nNow, the question is whether A is true or not. If A is true, then either B or F must be true. If A is false, then P1 is trivially true regardless of B and F.\n\nBut we don't have direct information about A, B, or F. However, premises 4 and 5 both relate to F. Premise 4 says that if earthworms have no lungs (E), then F → ¬D. Premise 5 says that if bombs are more likely outside (Bomb), then F → ¬D.\n\nPremise 3 is an either/or: E ∨ Bomb. So, at least one of E or Bomb must be true.\n\nNow, if E is true, then from premise 4, F → ¬D. If Bomb is true, then from premise 5, F → ¬D. Since E ∨ Bomb is true, either way, if F is true, then ¬D.\n\nSo, if F is true, then D is false.\n\nBut does F hold? From P1: A → (B ∨ F). If A is true, then at least one of B or F must be true. If A is false, then B ∨ F can be anything.\n\nHowever, premise 2 tells us that if P1 is true, then ¬D. So, if P1 is true, then ¬D. If P1 is false, then we don't get any information about D.\n\nBut we don't know if P1 is true or false. However, if P1 is true, then D is false. If P1 is false, then we don't know about D.\n\nBut wait, premise 3 says E ∨ Bomb. So, regardless of A, if F is true, then D is false. But we don't know if F is true.\n\nFrom P1: If A is true, then F could be true. If A is false, F could still be true or false.\n\nSo, the only way to guarantee D is false is if F is true. But we don't have enough information to confirm F is true.\n\nTherefore, it's uncertain whether D is false.\n</think>\n\nFinal answer: Uncertain", "insertion_step": 3, "domain": "logic", "eval_type": "truth_value", "dialogue_id": "122ef7b9-e56d-4367-ba9e-3841fc87bd15", "n_rounds": 1, "condition": "adversarial", "turn2_source": "baseline_prefix", "dialogue_mode": "adaptive_alternating", "recovery_terminated_by": "natural"}
